      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "Find the slope of $y=\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2}+2x-20)$ at $x=0.$\n\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by ${f}^{'}(0).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{'}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}u,\\,u={x}^{2}+2x-20.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x+2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}u\\cdot (2x+2) \\\\  & =(2x+2)\\operatorname{cos}u \\\\  & =(2x+2)\\operatorname{cos}({x}^{2}+2x-20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(0) & =(2\\cdot 0+2)\\operatorname{cos}({0}^{2}+2\\cdot 0-20) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}(-20) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is $2\\operatorname{cos}20.$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
        ":question/id": "4d354549-5407-518d-b3e2-fa2e33591dfe",
